#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "461c0f55-cb85-511e-b8cb-be11f8a3c250" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Reciprocal and Radical Components"}] :question/id #uuid "9a86e942-8852-5cce-b9be-e7a5eff46d12" :question/math-academy-id "e-12350" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle \\sqrt{t+3}, \\: t^3-t^2, \\: \\dfrac{1}{t+1}\\right\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{t+3}$ is $t \\in [-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3-t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\dfrac{1}{t+1}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/9577ea515e58e4db49ee5a5ef9f923bc8b34e2dc232b904c956cc1b778141f14.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in [-3,-1)\\cup(-1,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"}] :question/id #uuid "726fa7f3-a110-5730-a734-1019fd77de9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12362" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = \\ln{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{1}{t-2} \\, \\mathbf{j} + e^{2t} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\ln{t}$ is $t \\in (0, \\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\dfrac{1}{t-2}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,2)\\cup(2,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{2t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/2a4931e8dc80192219736fc4dbc586ad2405bbb628d75bd0e17fa9616567d793.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/id #uuid "a63854e6-412b-5308-be28-c05337aa4b9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12455" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf f (t) = \\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right) \\, \\mathbf i + t^3 \\,\\mathbf j + e^t \\,\\mathbf k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[5pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\tan\\left(t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nt =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t^3$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $e^{t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty,\\infty), \\quad t \\neq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + n\\pi},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03709d7c-ec71-5c1f-a495-0740bf258a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294893} {:answer/id #uuid "cc457de1-b177-5da1-93fc-53544904798f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294894} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8c4219-33e4-5c5b-9540-3e720a487d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294895} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdacc83-ed64-527a-86d0-c189ee8f8f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294896} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ef78a2-40bf-5bdc-8081-64f1c89fa3e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ef78a2-40bf-5bdc-8081-64f1c89fa3e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294897} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1feb855-3e22-5148-b879-1eff2fac7416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5d1e81f-1c74-5084-a455-1824f15d416a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162635" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)={e}^{1-t}\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1)\\,j+t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${e}^{1-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c99ec3d-24ad-5708-98ef-49ff306f5957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294900} {:answer/id #uuid "83aaf53b-9232-5b6e-acd6-ca1c9b6b7da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294901} {:answer/id #uuid "400b5091-d619-5e46-83e3-175580a9b4b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294902} {:answer/id #uuid "3a95a3ec-b3f6-5720-b6b8-34ed1eb16598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294903} {:answer/id #uuid "f02deb10-cfd1-5f69-974a-dfd0ffbbd1b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a95a3ec-b3f6-5720-b6b8-34ed1eb16598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294903} :answer-field/id #uuid "c803f1f9-c1eb-5283-8607-56786b7b617f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef60da03-fa45-5a6d-8813-3f716be9505a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162646" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+({t}^{3}+1)\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n2t=\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${t}^{3}+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "807b66eb-2b9c-5971-9e8f-3c1771e54d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294914} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ddec81-dee5-5635-a451-6a804d027ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294915} {:answer/id #uuid "941bda75-5e65-5307-a7ea-ae4cbff001d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294916} {:answer/id #uuid "da6240d0-4a37-5a65-bb5f-6acd2f75d852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294917} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf1a2c7-3052-535e-8a9f-279102dc486d" :answer/type 